\f0\fs24 \cf0 It is 11:10 pm, and finally, I am in my room, resting. Whether I am in a position to process the weight of everything that has transpired, I truly doubt. Perhaps I will simply fall asleep with a smile, holding a quiet gratitude to the universe for making me its instrument\'97and for gifting me a family and friends who nourish me with such profound love.\
\
There is a vastness here that feels unprocessable for now. Maybe it will settle in time, or perhaps over the next seven days, if the silence allows it.\
I haven't mentioned this yet, as today was consumed by the rush of preparation, but early this morning I reached out to \'c1ngel Antonio\'97the director of the museum and the monk whose presence I find truly angelic. I proposed that I spend six hours each day, from 7:30 am to 1:30 pm, painting in absolute silence.\
\
I will not be speaking; I will simply be existing and creating within the gallery. The works will be abstract, born from the textures and inspirations of this place. In the spirit of an offering, I am donating everything I create over the next week to the museum. My only request was for them to provide the supplies. They graciously covered the costs, and today I visited the shop to gather my materials. I was judicious with the spending, though these brands and materials are entirely new to me. I am curious to see what will emerge from them.\
\
So, tomorrow begins this ritual: six hours of silent painting every day. It was a choice I made for myself; I cannot remain idle. Furthermore, I have invited the public to join. If anyone wishes to observe, they may view it as a piece of performance art. If they feel moved to create, they are welcome to bring their own materials and paint alongside me in the same room.\
\
One local painter was so moved by the idea that he has already promised to come this Friday. Many found it hard to believe I was allowing such access. So often, artists shroud their work in mystery, making the act of creation feel inaccessible and guarded. I want to do the opposite\'97to make art approachable, open, and shared.\
So, this is the path for the coming days, my dear friends. Thank you for being there. \uc0\u10084 \u65039 }